Yeah. Tolkien's world had a linguistic and mythological depth that I
don't
think has ever been matched. On most every other level it's lacking.
The
lack of wild animals, the economic impossibility of it all, things
like that.

In the sub-genre of richly detailed created worlds, I suspect that
Robert
Jordan is unsurpassed. The detail of the world, from trade and
economics
to what style of dresses people wear, is incredible. I would love, one
day,
for some of his notes to be released to the public. Tolkien's
languages are
mainly known from material that was never published, iirc.

There isn't much I can see from RJ that would add to Nethack, however.
Not
much of the flavour, or characters. There are some ter'angreal
(angreal and
sa'angreal only give the user access to more of the power) that might
make
good artefacts. Maybe. Trollocs wouldn't add anything (gameplay wise
they'd
be equal to orcs, basically) except maybe hallucination messages.
Myrdraal
(spelling?) might be interesting, but again are probably covered by
existing
monsters.

If you wanted to do anything with the whole magic system, that's a
huge thing,
and would be an entirely different game.

